    {
      "slug": "werl-triptych",
      "title": "Werl Triptych",
      "year": 1438,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/Werl-Triptychons.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 2700,
      "imageHeight": 2952,
      "story": "The Werl Triptych is a triptych altarpiece completed in Cologne in 1438, of which the centre panel has been lost. The two remaining wings are now in the Prado in Madrid. It was long attributed to the Master of Flémalle, now generally believed to have been Robert Campin, although this identity is not universally accepted. Some art historians believe it may have been painted as a pastiche by either the workshop or a follower of Campin or the Master of Flémalle.",
      "facts": [
        "The room is obviously from a contemporary middle-class rather than biblical setting, and contains many of the same details found in the centre panel of the c 1425–28 Mérode Altarpiece, also attributed to Robert Campin.",
        "He probably commissioned this work for the Minorite church in Cologne.",
        "The painting is typical of early commissioned altarpieces in that the donor is not included in the central devotional scene."
      ],
      "wikipediaUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Werl_Triptych",
